      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "This study was a head-to-head, intraindividual comparison of the diagnostic accuracy and side effect profile of bolus and infusion administration of adenosine for stress myocardial contrast echocardiography (MCE)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Adenosine MCE was performed in 64 subjects, referred for stress thallium-201 single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) for known or suspected CAD. Each patient received adenosine by multiple boluses (6-12 mg/bolus) and infusion (140 mug/kg per min for 6 min) forms in random order, given at least 20 minutes apart."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "No prolonged or serious adverse events occurred during either adenosine bolus or infusion. Compared to SPECT imaging, the sensitivity, specificity, and concordance for the diagnosis of CAD were 77%, 87%, and 82% for adenosine infusion MCE and 81%, 90%, and 86% for adenosine bolus MCE, respectively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Both adenosine infusion and adenosine bolus protocols are safe for MCE in humans and can be used for the diagnosis of CAD."
        }
      ]},
